00:00:00.000 --> 99:59:59.999
Phase 3, Data engineering fundamentals. Data work is not the boring part before the model. It is the part that decides whether the model can work at all, in training and at 3 a.m. in production. This phase turns a CSV into a pipeline you can trust. This phase has 6 lessons. Lesson 1: Profile the data. Quality usually matters more than model choice. Inspect meaning, units, missingness, duplicates, category drift, and the way labels were created. The question for this lesson: What does this dataset really contain?. Data is not messy because someone lied. It is messy because the world is messy and every collection process leaves fingerprints. Before modeling, become a detective: who collected this, when, why, and what does one row actually represent? Missingness is a clue, not just an inconvenience. A column missing more often for new customers may be telling you about the signup flow. A sudden change in category spelling may be telling you the upstream form changed. You will learn: Missingness can be signal, random noise, or a collection failure., Duplicates and identifiers can leak information., A target distribution sets expectations for metrics and baselines.. Practice: "Produce a data report with schema. Watch out for: Dropping nulls without measuring the impact., Treating IDs as ordinary features., Ignoring changes in how the data was collected.. You are ready when: Document every column., Measure nulls and duplicates., Inspect target balance.. Recommended video: Complete Python Pandas data science tutorial. Lesson 2: Split data honestly. A split estimates future behavior. Time, groups, repeated users, and leakage determine whether a random split is honest. The question for this lesson: Will this validation result survive deployment?. A split is a time machine. You hide part of the present, train on the rest, and ask the model to predict the hidden part. If the hidden part is secretly similar to the training part, the machine is lying to you. Random splits assume rows are independent. Customers, sessions, and days usually are not. When the future matters, split by time. When the same person appears twice, split by person. The rule is simple: the validation setup should look like deployment, not like a shuffled spreadsheet. You will learn: Training fits parameters; validation selects decisions; test is the final audit., Group splits protect repeated entities such as customers or sessions., Time splits simulate the future when patterns or labels evolve.. Practice: "Create random. Watch out for: Preprocessing the full dataset before the split., Tuning repeatedly on the test set., Splitting rows instead of customers or sessions.. You are ready when: Define independence., Freeze a test set., Keep transforms inside folds.. Recommended video: Machine Learning Fundamentals: Cross Validation. Lesson 3: Engineer features safely. Feature engineering is domain reasoning plus strict time discipline. Every transform must be reproducible in training and serving. The question for this lesson: Can the model see useful signal at prediction time?. Features are the model's senses. If the signal is not in the features at prediction time, no algorithm can invent it. A brilliant model staring at a useless column is just an expensive random number generator. The dangerous part is time. A feature that looks predictive in training may be a ghost from the future: a cancellation reason, a post-purchase survey, a label that only exists after the outcome. If it will not exist at serving time, it does not exist. You will learn: Encode dates, text, categories, and numbers inside a pipeline., Interactions must earn their complexity through validation., Feature source and timing are part of the specification.. Practice: "Create three features. Watch out for: Fitting encoders globally., Unsafe target encoding., Features that are unavailable at serving time.. You are ready when: Document feature timing., Fit transforms inside folds., Measure marginal value.. Recommended video: "Decision Trees. Lesson 4: Test data and pipelines. Data contracts and pipeline tests catch the failures that model metrics cannot: schema changes, impossible ranges, bad transforms, and serving mismatch. The question for this lesson: What breaks before a score moves?. A model that works only on your laptop is a demo. Tests are how you keep it working after you forget why it worked, after a dependency upgrades, and after a well-meaning teammate renames a column. You do not need a giant test suite. You need a few sharp ones: a schema check, an edge-case transform check, a known-prediction check, and a leakage check. Those catch the failures that quietly ruin a score. You will learn: Unit tests verify local transforms., Data tests guard schema, range, null rate, and freshness., Golden examples prevent behavior regressions.. Practice: "Write one schema test. Watch out for: Testing imports only., Exact float assertions everywhere., Different train and serve transforms.. You are ready when: Test edge inputs., Run tests in CI., Keep a golden dataset.. Recommended video: Python Testing: A Beginner's Guide to Pytest. Lesson 5: Track experiments. A run is code, data, parameters, metrics, artifacts, and a decision. Tracking converts trial-and-error into evidence. The question for this lesson: Can you explain why this model exists?. An experiment without a record is a rumor. Three weeks later, nobody remembers which preprocessing changed, which split was used, or why the second-best run was abandoned. The model still works, but the reasoning is gone. A run should answer a question. Name it after the question, log the data version and parameters, save the full pipeline, and write one sentence about what you learned. The goal is not a tidy dashboard; it is a trail you can follow backward. You will learn: Log parameters, metrics, plots, dataset version, and artifacts together., Name runs after the question being tested., Persist the complete preprocessing-plus-model pipeline.. Practice: "Run three intentional experiments. Watch out for: Logging only the final score., Comparing mismatched datasets., Model artifacts with no lineage.. You are ready when: Record data version., Log parameters., Save the full artifact.. Recommended video: MLflow: A Quickstart Guide. Lesson 6: NumPy, pandas, and SQL. Use arrays for numeric work, dataframes for tabular transforms, and SQL for joins, aggregation, and the data that never fits in memory. The question for this lesson: Can you answer a data question without looping over every row?. NumPy gives you vectorized math and predictable shapes. pandas gives you labelled columns, joins, groupbys, and missing-data tools. SQL gives you the same ideas where the data actually lives, with a planner that can be much faster than pulling everything into Python. The skill is translation: a filter is a WHERE clause, a groupby is GROUP BY, a join is a join. Learn to express the question in the tool that owns the data, then verify row counts before trusting the result. You will learn: Vectorized operations replace most Python loops over numeric data., Join keys and cardinality determine whether a merge explodes rows., SQL NULL, pandas NaN, and zero are three different ideas.. Practice: Answer five questions about one dataset twice: once with pandas and once with SQL. Reconcile any differences before writing the model.. Watch out for: Looping over dataframe rows for ordinary arithmetic., Joining without checking duplicate keys., Treating NULL as zero or silently dropping it.. You are ready when: Check shapes after every merge., Use explicit dtypes., Reconcile pandas and SQL totals.. A clean dataset, an honest split, and a pipeline that reruns the same way twice. Take your time with each lesson and practice the examples.
